<%#
  Attached design files, grouped by role into COLLAPSIBLE sections in ROLE_LABELS order.

  `@attachments` has already had the dead ones filtered out by ProjectsController#show, against
  `@page_nodes` (PageNodes) — a file deleted from the library leaves the project immediately, and
  `@missing_attachment_count` is how many did, so the change is never silent. There is no "(file
  unavailable)" tile any more: it was the only thing that branch rendered, and hiding those rows
  makes it unreachable rather than merely rare. That partition is also why `_attachment_group`
  dereferences `@page_nodes[...]` with no `&.` — every row reaching it has a live node.

  `@page_thumbs` (ThumbnailMap) is best-effort and INDEPENDENT OF THAT, which is the distinction to
  keep: a backend blip degrades a tile to its format badge, a different condition from the file
  being gone. It is exactly why liveness reads `nodes.present` and not the port — ThumbnailMap
  rescues FetchError to {}, so an unmounted NAS would otherwise empty this panel and report every
  file deleted. Pinned by page_nodes_test.rb.

  ONE `thumbs` controller wraps every group rather than one per grid: Stimulus finds targets
  anywhere in the controller element's subtree, so a single instance polls all pending cells on the
  page instead of N instances polling their own slice. `filemenu` rides along on the same element
  for the same reason — one document-level listener, every tile.

  NATIVE <details>, NOT A STIMULUS CONTROLLER: collapsing is what the element is for, it works
  before any JavaScript loads, and `models/_file_tree.html.erb` already uses it for exactly this.
  Open by default, because the panel's job is to show a project's files.
%>
<section class="panel attachments-panel">
  <div class="panel-head">
    <h2>Files</h2>
    <div class="panel-actions">
      <%# "Look at my library NOW", instead of waiting on library_scan's 15 minutes (share/s3) or
          remote_changelog_pull's 2 (chunk_remote). It scans the LIBRARY, not this folder — a scan
          cannot be aimed at a subfolder (Library::Reconciler compares against the whole catalog), so
          the door refreshes the store and the existing chains do the per-project filing. %>
      <%= button_to "Scan folder", project_scan_path(@project), method: :post, class: "linkbtn" %>
      <%= link_to "Attach existing", new_project_attachment_path(@project), class: "linkbtn" %>
      <%= render "upload_control", upload_url: project_uploads_path(@project),
                 attach_url: project_attachments_path(@project), redirect_url: project_path(@project),
                 parent_node_id: @upload_parent_node_id %>
    </div>
  </div>

  <%# A share/s3 scan is a BACKGROUND job — an unbounded NAS walk or bucket listing — so the page
      that started it cannot show the result. One reload, to the param-free url, which is what
      stops it looping. Rendered only on the async path: chunk_remote finished inside the request.
      §14d — a data-controller name that resolves to nothing is a SILENT no-op, which is why
      app/javascript/controllers/scanreload_controller.js must exist and stimulus_hygiene_test
      guards that it does. %>
  <% if @scan_started && capabilities.scannable? %>
    <span data-controller="scanreload"
          data-scanreload-url-value="<%= project_path(@project) %>"></span>
  <% end %>

  <% if @attachments.empty? && @missing_attachment_count.zero? %>
    <p class="muted">No files attached yet.</p>
  <% else %>
    <%# Group by role, but fold anything off-vocabulary into "other" rather than dropping it. Every
        writer of `role` currently routes through role_for (total) or the model validation, so this
        cannot trigger today — but the render iterates ROLE_LABELS, so without this an unrecognized
        role renders NOWHERE. A file silently vanishing from a user's project is a far worse failure
        than one filed under the wrong heading, and role_for's own unknown-extension default makes
        "other" the obvious bucket.

        BACKUPS COME OUT FIRST, and they are keyed on the NODE's name rather than the attachment's
        `label`, because the name is what LightBurn wrote and the label is whatever a user may since
        have typed. They keep their own section at the bottom instead of being dropped: a rescue copy
        is still a real file in the project's folder, and a panel that pretended otherwise would be
        lying about what is on disk. %>
    <% backups, live = @attachments.partition { |a| Maker::FileAttachment.backup?(@page_nodes[a.node_id].name) } %>
    <% grouped = live.group_by { |a| Maker::FileAttachment::ROLE_LABELS.key?(a.role) ? a.role : "other" } %>
    <div data-controller="thumbs filemenu" data-thumbs-url-value="<%= thumbnails_files_path %>">
      <% Maker::FileAttachment::ROLE_LABELS.each do |role, label| %>
        <% group = grouped[role] %>
        <% next if group.blank? %>
        <%= render "attachment_group", key: role, label: label, group: group, open: true %>
      <% end %>

      <%# COLLAPSED, and that is the whole of "don't show the backups": they are one click away
          rather than gone, so nothing about the project's folder is hidden from someone looking
          for it. %>
      <% if backups.any? %>
        <%= render "attachment_group", key: "backups", label: "Backups", group: backups, open: false %>
      <% end %>
    </div>

    <% if @missing_attachment_count.positive? %>
      <p class="muted attachments-missing">
        <%= pluralize(@missing_attachment_count, "attached file") %> no longer in your library.
        <%= link_to "Check the trash", trash_index_path %>
      </p>
    <% end %>
  <% end %>
</section>
